WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.118
Destination control protects both data and credentials.

2
00:00:03.118 --> 00:00:08.655
A secure client should know which schemes, hosts, ports, and paths are
allowed for a given integration.

3
00:00:08.655 --> 00:00:16.928
HTTPS should be the normal expectation for sensitive API communication,
and TLS certificate validation and hostname validation should remain
enabled.

4
00:00:16.928 --> 00:00:26.219
Disabling verification may make a connection work temporarily, but it
weakens the client's ability to confirm that it is communicating with the
intended trusted endpoint.

5
00:00:26.219 --> 00:00:29.464
Name resolution and address behavior matter at a high level.

6
00:00:29.464 --> 00:00:33.982
A configured host can resolve differently across environments, networks,
or time.

7
00:00:33.982 --> 00:00:44.673
Some applications need to consider private address ranges, loopback
addresses, metadata services, internal-only destinations, and public
endpoints when accepting destination-related input.

8
00:00:44.673 --> 00:00:54.601
The defensive principle is simple: do not allow untrusted input to decide
where sensitive requests or credentials are sent without validation
against an approved destination policy.

9
00:00:54.601 --> 00:00:56.956
Redirects should be handled deliberately.

10
00:00:56.956 --> 00:01:01.283
A redirect can move a request from one host, scheme, or trust boundary to
another.

11
00:01:01.283 --> 00:01:12.674
Clients need to decide whether redirects are allowed, which methods can
follow them, whether authorization headers or other sensitive headers
should be preserved, and what destination changes are acceptable.

12
00:01:12.674 --> 00:01:18.592
Blindly following redirects can leak metadata or credentials and can make
audit trails harder to understand.

13
00:01:18.592 --> 00:01:20.756
Proxies are also part of the trust model.

14
00:01:20.756 --> 00:01:29.665
Python clients and underlying libraries may use explicit proxy settings,
environment-driven proxy settings, service mesh behavior, or platform
egress controls.

15
00:01:29.665 --> 00:01:36.411
Those mechanisms can be useful for inspection, routing, and policy
enforcement, but they should be expected and documented.

16
00:01:36.411 --> 00:01:45.320
A secure client avoids surprising proxy behavior for sensitive calls and
logs enough destination and error context to support operations without
exposing secrets.
